--TEST--
`Core\Task\Channel::close` is the boundary `send` is asserted on from both sides: the send that fills the bound before it returns and is delivered, and the first send after it is a fatal no `catch` sees — ADR 0072 § 4 over `nvs_stdlib::channel`, `rule:errors/throwable-hierarchy`'s uncatchable tier
--FILE--
<?nvs
// The neighbouring cases pin what `close` does to values already queued and
// what the *capacity* does to a sender. Neither asks what happens to a `send`
// that arrives after the close, which is the one boundary of this class that
// has an answer a program cannot recover from.
//
// No tasks here on purpose: nothing below reaches the bound while the channel
// is open, so nothing below waits, and a case that needed a scheduler to
// observe a refusal would be pinning the scheduler as well.
var $chan = new Core\Task\Channel<int>(2);

// The accepting side. Two sends into a channel of two is the last value the
// bound takes without suspending — a third would wait for a consumer that does
// not exist here, so this is the bound reached exactly and not crossed.
$chan->send(1);
$chan->send(2);
$chan->close();

// Both of them really landed, rather than the sends having been accepted and
// dropped: the refusal below means nothing unless the accepted side is
// observed rather than assumed.
foreach ($chan as int $queued) {
    echo "delivered ", $queued, "\n";
}

// The refusing side. `send` is `void` and has no failure a caller could read,
// so a closed channel cannot answer "no" — and it must not answer "yes"
// either, because the value would be queued behind a stream that has already
// ended and no consumer will ever take it. That leaves the tier
// `rule:errors/throwable-hierarchy` reserves for a state with nothing true to report: a fatal,
// which is not a `Throwable` and which this `catch` therefore never sees.
try {
    $chan->send(3);
    echo "a closed channel accepted a send", "\n";
} catch (Throwable $never) {
    echo "unreachable: the refusal was caught", "\n";
}

echo "unreachable", "\n";
--EXPECT--
delivered 1
delivered 2
--EXPECT-ERROR--
FATAL: Core\Task\Channel::send was called on a closed channel
